nodecode-tracekit is a rewrite of Cygnux-Labs's Cygnux-Labs/Tracekit:

  https://github.com/Cygnux-Labs/Tracekit
  commit d41c68e335a0986100b8747729659798bcf9d949
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 Cygnux Labs (LICENSE)

The original is the Hermes Agent plugin in integrations/hermes (two hooks,
pre_tool_call and post_tool_call, over the tracekit-ai package's
tracekit.integrations.hermes) and the signer it talks to. What is rewritten
here is the plugin's behaviour in one Common Lisp cell: the same two hooks,
on Nodecode's :TOOL point, and the signer's wire spoken directly from Lisp —
one canonical-JSON frame per request over HTTP (POST /v2/rpc), the decision
(allow|deny|ask) consumed before a call runs, an ask waited out and its
approval withdrawn when nobody answers, and the outcome completed against the
decision with the result or its digest. The canonical JSON (RFC 8785) and the
sha256: event hash the signer binds are implemented in the cell, so no part
of the Python package is carried. The /tracekit command is the cell's own:
the original had no command, because `tracekit last` and `tracekit approvals`
are the CLI's.

What is left out, and why: the Python package tracekit-ai and its dependency
handling (the cell speaks the RPC itself); the autospawn of a same-user dev
signer on the first call (the cell expects a signer at the address the
section names, which keeps the signer the operator's own process); the Unix
socket and tcp:// transports (HTTP is the one a client off the signer's host
can use, and the signer serves loopback HTTP); one run per Hermes session
(Nodecode's :TOOL point hands a hook no session id, so one Nodecode process
is one run); Hermes's own hook timeout and tool deadline arithmetic
(ask_seconds is the cell's one bound); and Hermes's argument-type repair
(a call's arguments are decided as Nodecode parsed them).
